      "text": "For problem 49, Ben rolls 5 fair 12-sided dice. The even numbers on 1 die are $2,4,6,8,10,12$. There are 3 even numbers on 1 single die.\nThe total number of outcomes for 5 dice rolls are $$12^5 = 248832$$\nNow, for a single die there are 3 even numbers and 9 odd numbers. There are $\\binom{3}{2} = \\frac{3!}{2!(3-2)!} = 3$ ways to get exactly two evens and the probability of rolling even is $\\frac{3}{12} = \\frac{1}{4}$. \nThe probability of three dice being odd is $$\\left(\\frac{9}{12}\\right)^3 = \\frac{729}{1728}.$$ The probability of the remaining dice being even is $\\frac{3}{12} = \\frac{1}{4}$. Next, there are $\\binom{3}{1} = \\frac{3!}{1!(3-1)!} = 3$ ways to choose 1 even number.\nTherefore, the probability of 3 dice being odd and 2 dice being even is $$\\left(\\frac{729}{1728}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^2\\left(3\\right)$$ $$=\\frac{3^3 \\cdot 729}{12^4}$$ $$=\\frac{177147}{20736}$$\nThe combined probability of this happening is $$\\frac{3}{12} \\left(\\frac{177147}{20736}\\right)$$ $$=\\frac{177147}{82944}.$$\nThe final answer is \\boxed{\\frac{177147}{82944}}.",
